# One construction lifecycle on the production Kingdom Quickstart road (ZAP-034 root ruling):
# real Quickstart boot, the same production quote/CanPay/commission Quickstart already drives
# during boot (Harness/KingdomQuickstartBuildTest.cs), then -- uniquely for this command --
# further AutoRunner lines that spend real engine turns to carry that exact paid job to a
# finished building, and a real save.
#
# WHY THE QUICKSTART ROAD, NOT founding-first-city (see lifecycle-founding-road-refusal.persona).
# On founding-first-city, `realize` places no camp kit and dedicates no stockpile: dedication is a
# founder-interactive act (Core/KingdomCharterPart.Vessels.cs) a sealed script never drives, so
# lifecycle-open there can only wait its bounded budget and honestly refuse.
# Harness/KingdomQuickstartLifecycleProvider.cs's own docstring gives the ORIGINAL reason that
# road was chosen instead of Quickstart: "A Quickstart run carries no scenario auto-runner ...
# Nothing in a Quickstart run can therefore spend engine turns." That was true until this
# persona's own wiring: Harness/KingdomQuickstartLifecycleRunnerPatch.cs now adds the runner to a
# Quickstart game, but ONLY for the separate quickstart-lifecycle command (never quickstart-boot,
# quickstart-save or quickstart-build), and Harness/KingdomScenarioAutoRunner.cs skips its usual
# re-strip and its usual line-0 verb for that same command -- the Quickstart camp's real founder
# stock must never be obliterated, and the command's own first line is boot syntax, not a verb.
# The root ruling overrides the original docstring reason because it targets the Quickstart
# STOCKPILE lifecycle by name: on this road the stockpile IS dedicated, by
# World/KingdomQuickstartBootstrap.Materials.cs:34, before the AutoRunner ever starts, so
# lifecycle-open's CanPay reads a real dedication and proceeds instead of waiting.
#
# NO lifecycle-build HERE. Quickstart's own boot-time build phase already ran the exact
# quote/CanPay/commission sequence for the lifecycle command (Harness/KingdomQuickstartBuildTest.cs,
# gated on KingdomQuickstartBootTest.LifecycleRequested) and persisted its job id into the SAME
# durable key KingdomQuickstartLifecycleSteps.Build publishes
# (KingdomQuickstartLifecycleSteps.JobKey) -- running lifecycle-build again would only refuse
# "this lifecycle already commissioned its one job". Tools/check-quickstart-lifecycle.py's own
# judge() already reads the QUICKSTART-BUILD-* rows this boot phase journals for the quote and
# paid-commission links (it prefers whichever named group has more matches present), so nothing
# needs to be re-driven.
#
# THE ADVANCE BUDGET (native run 17, f691ab4): the Quickstart bootstrap stakes two tentrow plots
# (1700 ticks each) before the fire commission (900 ticks) this persona pays for, and the
# settlement's ONE raising gang (Growth/KingdomConstructionPresenceRules.cs:41-59) works only the
# single oldest active raising at a time -- so fire waits behind both tentrows: 2x1700 + 900 =
# 4300 labour-ticks of serial contention, plus the first of the settlement's ~1200-tick daily
# passes prices its own interval at 0 (no witness yet to price it against). That is at least
# ceil(4300/1200) + 1 = 5 passes before fire could even finish being worked, so a 2400-turn
# advance (2 passes) was structurally short regardless of the harness reading bug below. This
# advances 7200 turns (~6 daily passes, one turn ~= one tick per native run 13/17 evidence) --
# margin above the stated 5-pass minimum, still comfortably under
# KingdomScenarioAdvance.MaxTurns/persona_matrix.COUNTED_VERBS[advance] (10000).
#
# THE HARNESS READING FIX (native run 17): lifecycle-grown's own stall classification
# (Harness/KingdomQuickstartLifecycleStall.cs) used to read RemainingTicks/LastWorkedTick off
# r_KingdomScaffold unconditionally; a "fire" commission is r_KingdomPlotWorks, whose progress
# lives in KingdomPlots.PlotWork{Remaining,LastTick,Required}Property instead, so the scaffold
# read was always absent (-1/-1) and every plot-backed refusal read "no-labour-ever" regardless of
# whether labour had actually run. Now reads whichever lane Job.Projection names. The stamped
# lifecycle-grown row stays Bounded to 300 chars either way (KingdomScenarioRowValidator.
# MaxTextChars); the untruncated reading (selectedId=/candidates=/roots=/free=/plotRemaining=/
# lastSemanticTick=/schema=) lands in its own lifecycle-grown-detail bookkeeping row.
#
# Nothing is fabricated: no stock is minted and no job is forced into a phase. lifecycle-grown
# refuses honestly when the turn budget expires before the building stands, exactly as on the
# founding road.
#
# NATIVE RUN 36 (13122f0): THE FOUNDER DIED MID-ADVANCE. Boot, build and lifecycle-open landed
# clean, then during `advance 7200` a wandering creature bit the founder (the player body, standing
# on the rite cell with nobody at the keyboard) to death; succession refused (HeirUnreachable);
# the engine lowered Game.Running and the AutoRunner, which wakes only on the player's own
# BeginTakeActionEvent, could never journal a terminal row: no advance-progress row, ~50 minutes of
# silence. Two harness-only changes, both disclosed here, nothing in production touched:
#   1. DEATH IS TERMINAL (Harness/KingdomScenarioAutoRunner.Death.cs). The runner now observes
#      AfterDieEvent through the same player registrar production's seal uses and lands
#      `SCRIPT-STOPPED DIED <deathCategory>; reason=...; founderCell=...; guard=...` before the
#      engine's death popup. Tools/check-quickstart-lifecycle.py reads that prefix as the distinct
#      FAIL class `founder-died` (failClass), which outranks every other verdict (a chain FAIL or the
#      BLOCKER the unreached links would otherwise read). EXPECTED AFTERMATH: Finish releases
#      Popup.Suppress, so the engine's death popup (GameObject.Die -> ShowDeathMessage) then BLOCKS
#      the process on a keypress nobody sends; that frozen death screen is not a new hang - the
#      terminal row is already on disk and the driver reaps the process on it. The driver that reads
#      the row is Tools/run-personas.sh (persona_matrix.terminal_row); Tools/run-scenario.ps1 does
#      not parse SCRIPT-STOPPED/SCRIPT-COMPLETE at all, so a run-scenario.ps1 operator must read the
#      journal's last row themselves.
#   2. THE FOUNDER IS NOT A COMBAT TARGET DURING THE SCRIPTED ADVANCE
#      (Harness/KingdomScenarioFounderGuard.cs). For this command only (LifecycleRequested), from
#      the moment `advance` arms until it ends by any route, the guard raises the engine's own
#      XRLCore.IgnoreMe (decompile 2.0.211.51 XRL/Core/XRLCore.cs:217, the flag the engine's
#      `ignoreme` wish toggles, Wishing.cs:3396-3398): Brain.WantToKill pushes no Kill goal against
#      the player (Brain.cs:1075-1077) and GameObject.IsHostileTowards answers false toward the
#      player (GameObject.cs:11431-11433). It is NOT invulnerability - TakeDamage/Die never read
#      it, spawning is untouched, XRLCore.Calm and IDKFA are not used, the flag is NonSerialized
#      and the prior value is restored at release. Two bookkeeping `advance-guard` rows (start/end)
#      record founderCell=x,y, the guard state, and ignoreMe=; the advance-guard start row lands
#      just before the `advance OK` row because the flag must be up before the wait's first spend.
#      Both rows land ONLY when the guard armed: every other road's journal carries no advance-guard
#      row (Tools/upgrade_profile_witnesses.py reads the stage-source journal as an exact sequence).
#      A pump exception lowers the flag in a finally; a script stop cancels the wait and the guard.
#      LIMIT: the preferred "walk the founder into the camp tent / heart interior first" is inert
#      at Camp stage - the tent rows are staked, unwalled plots and the heart has no interior cell
#      yet - so the founder stays on the cell the boot left him on (walk=none in the row).
#
# NATIVE RUN 38 (2fa563c): lifecycle-grown built=1 for the first time, then lifecycle-save refused
# "more than one dedicated stockpile stands here". Both stores are lawful production artefacts and
# both are kept: the Quickstart bootstrap's camp materials chest (World/KingdomQuickstartBootstrap.
# Materials.cs:34; the store lifecycle-open read, id 495 in that run) and the heart's own dry store
# r_KingdomHeartStockpile (RuntimeData/ObjectBlueprints.xml KingdomStockpile=1; Architecture/
# KingdomArchitectures-CivicFaith.xml palette civic-heart-natural slot "store"), stamped when the
# settlement raised heart rung 1 (heartbasin) during the 7200-turn advance (Player.log "heart rung
# raised: 1 (heartbasin)"). Nothing is deleted or undedicated. The harness fault was binding to a
# SCAN ("exactly one") instead of to the store it opened with: lifecycle-open now records the store
# in its durable opened receipt (zone|storeId|turns, a string game state that survives the save),
# every later step - build, save, and session 2's loaded/next - resolves THAT id among the stores
# standing (Harness/KingdomQuickstartLifecycleStoreRules.cs, value-tested: ambiguity with a bound
# id resolves, without one refuses, a bound store that no longer stands refuses), and every step's
# row carries storeId=<id>; stores=<count>. Tools/check-quickstart-lifecycle.py reports storeIds and
# fails the chain as store-drift if the OK steps ever name different stores.
#
# NOT YET NATIVELY RUN with this budget: written and gate.sh-compiled only. The cold-load session
# and the next action after it are NOT in this script; see Tools/check-quickstart-lifecycle.py for
# why those two links still read BLOCKER without it.
DESCRIPTION=on the real Quickstart road the stockpile is already dedicated at boot, so lifecycle-open proceeds straight to a real engine-turn build (budgeted past the one-gang tentrow contention) and save
REQUEST=founding-first-city
SCRIPT=quickstart-lifecycle marsh yes;stagedigest;lifecycle-open;advance 7200;lifecycle-grown;lifecycle-save;stagedigest
VERBS=lifecycle-open,lifecycle-grown,lifecycle-save
EXPECT=QUICKSTART-BOOT-BEGIN:OK,QUICKSTART-BOOT-OBSERVED:OK,QUICKSTART-BOOT-COMPLETE:OK,QUICKSTART-BUILD-BEGIN:OK,QUICKSTART-BUILD-QUOTE:OK,QUICKSTART-BUILD-CANPAY:OK,QUICKSTART-BUILD-COMMISSION:OK,QUICKSTART-LIFECYCLE-PROFILE:OK,QUICKSTART-BUILD-COMPLETE:OK~commissioned=true,stagedigest:OK~founded=true,lifecycle-open:OK~step=startup,advance:OK,lifecycle-grown:OK~step=engine-turn-build,lifecycle-save:OK~step=save,stagedigest:OK~founded=true,COMPLETE
SET=growth,construction,native-regression
TIMEOUT=3000
